 and two datasets, we show that genre can be extracted with F1-scores of up to 98% and 71% using scikit-learn classifiers. Across both datasets, results consistently outperform the control task, providing a proof of concept that text genres can be inferred from LLMs with shallow learning models.\nL18: Comments:  | 13 pages, 5 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | cs.LG\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2511.16540 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2511.16540v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16540†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Nicky Pochinkov [cite14†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 16:53:12 UTC (1,094 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Beyond Tokens in Language Models: Interpreting Activations through Text Genre Chunks, by \\'Elo\\\"ise Benito-Rodriguez and 3 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite9†View PDF L36:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite15†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L45: \nL46: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite22†cs L51: cite23†cs.LG L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL7